    "tasks": [
      {
        "question": "what is an oribi?",
        "prediction": "An oribi (*Ourebia ourebi*) is a small, slender antelope found in eastern, southern, and western Africa. It is the sole member of its genus and was first described by the German zoologist Eberhard August Wilhelm von Zimmermann in 1782. There are eight identified subspecies.\n\nPhysically, the oribi features a slightly raised back, a long neck, and long limbs. Its coat is glossy and ranges from yellowish to rufous brown, contrasting with white on the chin, throat, underparts, and rump. The bushy tail is brown to black on the outside with white insides (though the subspecies *O. o. hastata* has a completely black tail). Only males possess horns; these are thin, straight, smooth at the tips, and ringed at the base. The species is sexually dimorphic, with males being slightly smaller than females in most subspecies, except for *O. o. ourebi*, where females are smaller.\n\nDietarily, the oribi is primarily a grazer, with grasses constituting up to 90% of its diet. It prefers fresh grasses such as *Andropogon*, *Eulalia*, *Hyparrhenia*, *Loudetia*, *Pennisetum*, and *Themeda* species, but also browses occasionally on flowers and *Boletus* mushrooms. They visit mineral licks regularly and tend to congregate in groups during the rainy season when grasses are abundant.\n\nThe oribi is typically diurnal and is classified as Least Concern by the IUCN, although one subspecies, *O. o. haggardi*, is listed as Vulnerable. Threats include poaching, agricultural expansion, and competition from livestock.",
